              "text": "In both ancient mythology and modern times, **ambrosia is most commonly classified as food**, while its counterpart, **nectar, is classified as drink**. \n\nHowever, the answer has a bit of nuance depending on the context:\n\n### 1. In Greek Mythology (The Origin)\n* **The Standard View:** In classical Greek mythology, ambrosia is the **food** of the gods, and nectar is their **drink**. Consuming them granted the gods their immortality and youth. \n* **The Nuanced View:** Ancient writers were not always consistent. In Homer\u2019s poems, ambrosia is the food and nectar is the drink. However, other lyric poets like Sappho and Anaxandrides occasionally flipped the roles, describing ambrosia as the drink and nectar as the food. Furthermore, because both were often associated with honey, the distinction between whether they were solid foods or liquid drinks was sometimes blurred. \n\n### 2. In Modern Culinary Terms\n* **Ambrosia Salad:** In modern cooking, ambrosia is a classic American dessert salad. It is definitively a **food**, typically made with a mixture of whipped cream (or sour cream/yogurt), mandarin oranges, pineapple, shredded coconut, and miniature marshmallows. \n\n### Summary\nIf you are taking a quiz or looking for the general rule of thumb: **Ambrosia is food, and Nectar is drink.**",
